275	2018-05-30 15:56:00	2018-05-30 16:19:00	1	Chili	Zoe and Mike's recipe	Build a chili "cologne".  In a large pot on medium heat, add 2 tbspn olive oil, \r2 finely chopped large yellow onions and 3 grated garlic cloves.  Cook till the onions soften, about 5 minutes.\r\rAsk your butcher for 1 pound each of sirloin and chuck-cuts that provide a balance of meatiness and richness.  Brown the ground or cubed meat in the pot so it picks up savory flavors.\r\rYou could use beef or chicken stock as the simmering liquid for chili, but beer or wine lends serious depth.  Try a beer like Negra Modelo or even a chardonnay.  Twelve ounces will do the trick.  At this point you should also add the following: 2 cans Rotel tomatoes with chiles plus 6 oz. tomato paste.  2 tbspn of our homemade chili powder and (salt, if it is a beanless chili).\r\rIf you decide to use beans don't salt the chili yet.  Salt can turn the beans tough.  Mix bean varieties for visual pop.  Two 15 oz. cans beans.   Pick any two of the following varieties:  kidney, white, pink, black.  Drained and rinsed plus 1 cup water, beer, stock or tomatoes stir thoroughly.\r\rThe longer you cook the chilie the more the flavors intensify. An hour cooks the meat, but two to three is best.  Lower the heat to a simmer cover and stir often until done.\r\rSeason and serve with shredded cheddar cheese, sour cream, lime wedges, and pickled red onions.  And don't forget the corn bread.  Makes 4 to 6 servings.	CHILI	\N
278	2018-08-21 20:37:00	2021-03-13 15:46:00	8	Pain Ordinaire Careme	A Daily Loaf (recipe can also be cut in half)	Using an electric mixer with the flat beater, mix 4 cups of the flour with the yeast and hot water.  1 minute 45 seconds in the microwave works well.  Mix for 10 minutes, the batter will be smooth and pull away from the sides of the bowl.  Sprinkle in the salt and beat for another 30 seconds or so.\r\rKneading\rSwitch to the dough hook on the mixer.  Add the remaining 2 cups of flour a little at a time.  Then knead it in the mixer another 10 minutes.  The dough will be soft, but a few sprinkles of flour will let you shape it into a ball.\r\rFirst Rising\rPlace the dough in an oiled bowl, cover with plastic wrap, and let it rise for 2 hours.\r\rSecond Rising\rTurn back the plastic wrap, turn the dough onto a work surface and knead briefly for about three minutes.  Or you can just turn and knead it in the bowl.  Recover with wax paper and let it rise for 1 1/2 hours.\r\rShaping and Proofing\rTurn the dough onto a floured board and punch it down, divide into two pieces.  Grease either a dual bagette pan or a cookie sheet and sprinkle some corn meal on the pan.  Shape the loaves, turning under to stretch the top surface and let it proof for 1 hour.\r\rBake in a preheated oven at 450° for 25-30 minutes.	PAIN ORDINAIRE CAREME	0
